      "text": "Based on the problem, if the integer $a$ is divided by 15 to get a quotient $t$ and a remainder $7$, that can be written as $a = 15t + 7$. Using the formula for remainders, we get:\n\n$$ab = 15ab + 9ab7$$\n\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\n$$a = 5t + 2 \\cdot 3 + 7 = 5t + 11$$\n\nFurther simplifying, we have:\n\n$$a = 5t + 11$$\n\nNow, dividing $a$ by 5, we get:\n\n$$a = 5s + 1$$\n\nWe get:\n\n$$a = 5s + 1$$\n\nHere, $s = t$, so:\n\n$$a = s + 1$$\n\nThus, the remainder when $a$ is divided by 5 is 1. The sum of the remainders when the same integer is divided by 5 and by 3 is:\n\n$$(s + 1) + 12 = s + 11$$\n\nSince $t = s$, the remainder when $a$ is divided by 5 is the same as the remainder when $a$ is divided by 3, which is 11. Hence, the answer is:\n\n\\boxed{11}",
