      "text": "To solve this problem, we can start by writing an equation based on the given information:\n\n$$integer = 15(x + 1) + 7 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}$$\n\nWe can rewrite Equation 1 in the form:\n\n$$integer = 15x + 29$$\n\nNow, we want to find the remainders when the integer is divided by 3 and by 5. To do this, we can substitute the value of the integer from Equation 1 into the expressions for the remainders:\n\nRemainder when divided by 3: $(15x + 29) \\bmod 3$\n\nRemainder when divided by 5: $(15x + 29) \\bmod 5$\n\nTo find the value of $(15x + 29) \\bmod 3$, we can simplify the expression using the rule $(a + b) \\bmod n = ((a \\bmod n) + (b \\bmod n)) \\bmod n$:\n\n$$(15x + 29) \\bmod 3 = ((15x \\bmod 3) + (29 \\bmod 3)) \\bmod 3$$\n\nSince $15x$ is divisible by 3, $15x \\bmod 3 = 0$. And, $29 \\bmod 3 = 2$. So:\n\n$$(15x + 29) \\bmod 3 = (0 + 2) \\bmod 3 = 2$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the value of $(15x + 29) \\bmod 5$, we can use the same rule:\n\n$$(15x + 29) \\bmod 5 = ((15x \\bmod 5) + (29 \\bmod 5)) \\bmod 5$$\n\nSince $15x$ leaves a remainder of 0 when divided by 5, $15x \\bmod 5 = 0$. And, $29 \\bmod 5 = 4$. So:\n\n$$(15x + 29) \\bmod 5 = (0 + 4) \\bmod 5 = 4$$\n\nFinally, we can find the sum of the remainders when the integer is divided by 3 and by 5:\n\n$$2 + 4 = \\boxed{6}$$",
